   "text": {
    "L1": "Food does not spoil just because time passes. Tiny living things called microbes grow on food and spoil it.",
    "L2": "Microbes are too small to see without a microscope. They can be in air, water and soil, and on our hands.",
    "L3": "When microbes land on food, they can grow and multiply. That is why food changes.",
    "L4": "Microbes grow fastest when food is warm and moist. Warmth plus moisture makes growth quick.",
    "L5": "Spoiled food may smell bad, turn slimy, change colour or grow fuzzy mould. Do not taste it to check.",
    "L6": "Covering food, keeping it cool and dry, and washing our hands slow microbes down.",
    "L7": "Not all microbes are harmful. Helpful microbes make curd, make idli batter rise and help make compost.",
    "L8": "So the same idea explains both cases: microbes grow and change materials, but the result can help us or spoil food.",
    "L9": "Now tap the sign that can tell you food has spoiled without tasting it."
   },
